WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.840
Hello! In this video, I will show you how David&nbsp;
Colombo found a way to steal 55 Tesla cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:14.480
from around the world by exploiting insecure&nbsp;
default configuration of TeslaMate application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.240
and ignorance of Tesla car owners. Link to&nbsp;
the original blog post is in the description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.520
Before we start, we all know that Russia invaded&nbsp;
Ukraine and Ukrainians need our help. That's why I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:36.320
am donating the income from BBRE premium in March&nbsp;
to Ukraine and charities like Polish Red Cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.080
supporting people that fled the country.&nbsp;
100% of your subscription goes there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:48.480
I cover all the fees and the tax. I also put&nbsp;
links to direct donations in the description.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:57.760
TeslaMate is a third-party data logger for your&nbsp;
Tesla. It gives you information like location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:04.000
charging status, analytics and many more. It's&nbsp;
self-hosted - you need to deploy the code on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.960
your server using docker. It's not an official&nbsp;
Tesla application so after the installation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.440
TeslaMate will then save the API token&nbsp;
in the database. This will allow it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:26.960
to pull data about your car from the official&nbsp;
Tesla API. TeslaMate is exposed over port 4000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:35.520
If you were using a public IP address, everyone&nbsp;
can see the position and status of your car. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.960
quite bad but it becomes even worse. Because on&nbsp;
port 3000, there is an exposed Grafana instance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:51.040
It's an open source tool for visualizing data.&nbsp;
TeslaMate downloads the information about your car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.280
from Tesla API while Grafana visualizes&nbsp;
it for you. It makes things like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:04.240
history of your journeys or usual parking spots&nbsp;
available to anyone who finds your IP address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:12.480
This is a serious privacy breach but it's not the&nbsp;
worst case scenario because the attacker won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:20.960
steal or control your car. For that, the attacker&nbsp;
would need a Tesla API key which is stored in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:28.320
TeslaMate database. It would allow the attacker&nbsp;
to not only control the air conditioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:37.600
lights or the radio but also unlocking doors and&nbsp;
windows. Although the API key is not enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.240
remotely control the accelerator or steer the&nbsp;
car, the attacker could enable keyless driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.840
unlock the doors and drive away by himself.&nbsp;
It's that much easier that he knows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:59.680
precise location of the car. So leaking this token&nbsp;
would be really, really bad. Data from your Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:07.040
was stored in Postgres database. Grafana had&nbsp;
access to this database to create visualizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:14.720
The Tesla API key was stored there as well but&nbsp;
obviously, it was not a part of any visualization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:23.120
You could use Grafana Explore to write custom&nbsp;
queries and get the key but this required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.120
authentication to Grafana and only the Tesla&nbsp;
owner should have the password to access that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:37.360
However, by default Grafana is started&nbsp;
with admin:admin credentials. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.880
the user should change it after the&nbsp;
installation but many don't bother doing it.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:50.000
In this particular case, it was possible to&nbsp;
log in to Grafana with the default admin password.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.591
It allowed using Grafana Explore to make&nbsp;
a query to extract the Tesla API key.

00:03:56.914 --> 00:04:01.520
Then, you were able to unlock the car and&nbsp;
drive away with someone's Tesla.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:10.080
David found this bug when doing a security audit.&nbsp;
After that, he also scanned the Internet looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:17.760
for other TeslaMate instances exposed with the&nbsp;
default password. He found about 55 of them which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:25.120
means 55 cars that could have been stolen. He&nbsp;
coordinated the disclosure with Tesla car owners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:31.280
and revocation of the tokens with the Tesla&nbsp;
security team. You can read the whole story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:39.360
on the blog. TeslaMate is not an official Tesla&nbsp;
application so this bug is not Elon Musk's fault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.360
The responsibility for this vulnerability&nbsp;
is on TeslaMate developers who created the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.600
application with insecure defaults, as&nbsp;
well as on Tesla car owners who exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.320
the application to the public internet and&nbsp;
didn't change those default credentials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:05.200
As for the fix, in TeslaMate application the&nbsp;
Tesla API token is now encrypted in the database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.720
and Grafana doesn't have&nbsp;
the password to decrypt it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:15.600
Also, all dashboards and databases of TeslaMate&nbsp;
are now unavailable without authentication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:24.400
The fix was introduced in version 1.25.1. So&nbsp;
watch out what you expose to the Internet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:32.160
always change default passwords. If you want&nbsp;
to see a $12,000 SSRF in Grafana via a chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:39.360
of open redirects, check out this video on your&nbsp;
right. For now thank you for watching and goodbye!

